          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I smoke and I have been diagnosed with COPD.",
          "answer": "A COPD diagnosis means you have a long-term lung condition that makes it harder to breathe out air fully. It usually develops over years and typically involves breathlessness, a chronic cough, and sputum (phlegm) production. Tobacco smoking is the main cause in high-income countries. The General clinical reference on COPD explains this.\n\nThe COPD guideline in this corpus says that stopping smoking is the only intervention shown to change the rate at which lung function declines. For this reason, quitting is the most important step you can take. A guideline on heart disease, which is a different condition, notes that combined behavioral and drug support for quitting works better than advice alone. Your clinician can talk with you about which support options suit you. I can't recommend a specific product or dose.\n\nThe COPD guideline also lists other steps that matter:\n- **Vaccinations** against influenza, pneumococcal disease, COVID-19 and, where recommended, RSV reduce flare-ups (exacerbations).\n- **Pulmonary rehabilitation**, a supervised exercise and education programme, improves breathlessness and exercise ability more than inhalers do.\n- **Regular reviews** at least once a year, and after any flare-up or change in inhaler treatment, with a check of how you use your inhaler.\n\nI could not retrieve the detailed lifestyle guidance for COPD (diet, activity and sleep), so I can't cover those areas here. Your clinician or a pulmonary rehabilitation team can go through them with you.\n\nIf you have sudden worsening breathlessness, chest pain, blue lips, confusion, or coughing up blood, seek emergency care straight away.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
