          "answer": "Before I answer, I need to know what you mean by \"stopping.\" Could you tell me which behaviour, medication or treatment you mean, and which condition is involved? The answer depends on those details, and I'd rather not guess.\n\nIn general terms, stopping a risk factor or treatment can still change outcomes at many stages. Some changes help quickly. Others take months or years to show. Whether a change matters at a given stage depends on the condition and on what has already happened.\n\nFor COPD and smoking specifically, the COPD reference in this corpus says that stopping smoking is the main measure that helps. The COPD guideline says it is the only intervention shown to change how fast lung function declines. Stopping also reduces flare-ups (exacerbations), which the same reference describes as a way to slow the condition's progress. Those benefits apply at most stages of COPD, but the corpus does not say how much a person's stage changes the size of the benefit. Your clinician can tell you that for your situation.\n\nIf you have sudden worsening breathlessness, chest pain, blue lips, confusion, or coughing up blood, seek emergency care straight away.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
