          "question": "How much difference does it make to how breathless I get?",
          "answer": "The passages I retrieved do not explain how fitness, weight, smoking, medication, or an underlying condition change how breathless a person gets. I can't give a general answer on that from this material.\n\nSome related points do appear:\n- The heart failure reference lists breathlessness on exertion, then at lower effort, and waking at night breathless, as patterns that clinicians look for. It names coronary artery disease and long-standing high blood pressure as the most common causes.\n- The asthma reference says breathlessness and wheeze often vary and are often triggered by identifiable factors.\n- The COPD guideline says pulmonary rehabilitation, a supervised exercise and education programme, improves breathlessness and exercise ability more than inhalers do.\n\nTo make the answer specific, could you tell me which factor you mean? For example, your fitness level, your weight, smoking, a particular medicine, or a condition you have been diagnosed with.\n\nIf your breathlessness is sudden, is getting worse quickly, comes with chest pain, blue lips, confusion, or coughing up blood, or leaves you unable to speak in full sentences, seek emergency care straight away.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n\n[sources: condition-heart-failure, condition-asthma, guideline-copd-diagnosis-and-stepwise-management]",
